WEBVTT

00:00:00.215 --> 00:00:02.839
The aim is to control the cancer and help people live

00:00:02.839 --> 00:00:05.315
longer when surgery to remove it is not an option.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.062
Durvalumab, also called Imfinzi, is immunotherapy.

00:00:09.238 --> 00:00:14.058
Cancer cells can use a signal called PD-L1 to slow down immune cells.

00:00:14.232 --> 00:00:17.952
Durvalumab blocks that signal, helping immune cells attack cancer.

00:00:18.302 --> 00:00:21.380
The study behind this treatment is called TOPAZ-1.

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:23.962
Patients were assigned to treatment groups by chance.

00:00:24.138 --> 00:00:25.728
These are group results.

00:00:25.902 --> 00:00:28.048
Your team can explain what they mean for you.

00:00:28.398 --> 00:00:31.308
Your treatment card shows which schedule is yours.

00:00:31.484 --> 00:00:33.442
Day 1. All three drugs.

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:36.162
Day 8. Gemcitabine and cisplatin.

00:00:36.338 --> 00:00:38.388
The plan repeats every three weeks.

00:00:38.562 --> 00:00:41.544
A visit may move if you are unwell or need more tests,

00:00:41.544 --> 00:00:43.752
so use the date your day ward gives you.

00:00:44.103 --> 00:00:46.766
Allow extra time at the hospital for blood results,

00:00:46.766 --> 00:00:49.482
assessment, preparation and any further observation.

00:00:49.658 --> 00:00:53.500
At each visit, mention any new symptom, even if it has settled.

00:00:53.674 --> 00:00:56.824
It helps the team decide whether it is safe to give the next dose.

00:00:57.174 --> 00:01:01.700
This time you receive just durvalumab, through a drip lasting about an hour.

00:01:01.876 --> 00:01:04.410
The plan repeats every four weeks while the treatment

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.465
is helping and side effects are manageable.

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:09.776
Liver inflammation can first show up on a blood test.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.267
Repeat blood tests help spot changes early.

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:15.930
Your team may also check cortisol if a hormone problem is suspected.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.430
A reaction can happen during a drip or shortly afterwards.

00:01:19.606 --> 00:01:23.620
Tell the nurse immediately if you feel hot, shivery, itchy or dizzy,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.220
or if your throat feels tight or breathing becomes difficult.

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:31.034
Staff can pause the infusion, check you and treat the reaction.

00:01:31.384 --> 00:01:34.894
At home, you may notice tiredness, itchy skin,

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.786
a rash, loose stools, or aching muscles and joints.

00:01:38.961 --> 00:01:41.773
Ring your oncology team promptly about new symptoms.

00:01:41.948 --> 00:01:45.224
Reporting symptoms early gives your team time to find the cause.

00:01:45.574 --> 00:01:49.007
If you develop a fever or suddenly feel unwell, ring the 24-hour

00:01:49.007 --> 00:01:52.332
number straight away so the team can also check for infection.

00:01:52.507 --> 00:01:55.617
Ring your oncology team urgently, even between visits.

00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:59.779
Ring urgently if the whites of your eyes or your skin turn yellow,

00:01:59.779 --> 00:02:03.245
your urine becomes dark, or your belly becomes more swollen.

00:02:03.595 --> 00:02:08.170
Passing much less urine than usual or developing new swelling is another reason to ring.

00:02:08.345 --> 00:02:10.429
Your team can arrange the right tests.

00:02:10.779 --> 00:02:13.799
Sometimes the immune system inflames healthy organs too.

00:02:13.974 --> 00:02:17.744
A new cough or shortness of breath may be a sign of lung inflammation.

00:02:17.919 --> 00:02:21.345
For the bowel, ring about new diarrhoea, even if mild.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.014
Tell the team how often you are going and whether there is pain, blood or mucus.

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:30.511
Blisters, peeling skin or painful mouth sores need urgent assessment.

00:02:30.686 --> 00:02:34.076
Side effects can first appear months after your last infusion.

00:02:34.426 --> 00:02:38.676
Before you leave the day ward, save your hospital’s 24-hour oncology number.

00:02:38.851 --> 00:02:43.841
Carry your immunotherapy alert card and show it if you see a GP or an emergency team.

00:02:44.016 --> 00:02:48.346
If you feel unwell and cannot reach oncology, seek urgent medical assessment.

00:02:48.521 --> 00:02:54.875
For severe breathing difficulty, severe chest pain or collapse, call 112 or 999.
